From c3d272dea7569561191ca9bc0fd94fb52c802e45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:42:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: HDR10+ across pictures without metadata, as with
 Dolby Vision RPUs

A picture without HDR10+ metadata of its own cleared the InfoFrame, and
the next one with metadata sent it again: a stream that misses it now
and then would switch the sink between HDR10+ and HDR10 from one
picture to the next.

KODI::UTILS::HDR10PLUS::CGap gives each picture its InfoFrame with the
rule Hades has for the RPUs of Dolby Vision (CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg):
fewer than 12 pictures in a row without metadata keep the InfoFrame of
the last one that had it (the same scene); 12 leave HDR10+, and the
output is plain HDR10 until 48 pictures in a row have it again, twice as
many each time it leaves again, up to 1440. A stream whose first
pictures have none (fewer than 12) starts HDR10+ with the first that
has. A flush (a seek) drops what was held; a new stream starts from
the beginning. Both decoders that make the InfoFrame use it (FFmpeg and
DRM PRIME), and the log says when HDR10+ leaves and comes back.

Whether a stream may lack it: the AV1 one may not (HDR10+ AV1 Metadata
Handling Specification v1.0.1, 2.2.2: one HDR10+ metadata OBU for each
shown frame), and FFmpeg's HEVC decoder already gives the metadata of
the last SEI to the pictures after it until a flush (FFmpeg 9.0.2,
hevcdec.c: its SEI state is reset only at init, flush and close). So
this acts at the start of a stream, after a seek and where a stream
does without.

Host test (hdr10plus_gap_test.cpp): passes, fails against Kodi as it
was, and every mutation of the rule is caught.
